  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1239 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nAt midday today, a cutoff low was evident on satellite imagery\nover the Oklahoma Panhandle. This feature will weaken over the\nnext 24 hours as it tracks east-northeast across Kansas and\nnorthern Missouri. Mid-to upper-level flow across the CONUS will\nbecome much less amplified tomorrow, transitioning to weak west-\nto-northwest flow across the Mississippi River valley. A cold\nfront will move across the Mid-South during the day Friday.\nShowers and thunderstorms will develop tonight in a WAA regime\nahead of the front, increasing in coverage and spreading east\nthrough the night and Friday. Expect our first widespread and\nlong-duration rainfall event in quite a while Friday, with at\nleast a few showers lingering through the weekend. NBM\nprobability of 2 inches of rainfall or more tonight through\nSunday morning is between 10 and 35%, highest in northeast\nArkansas. For the event duration, the highest total rainfall\naccumulations, through early Monday, are expected in northeast\nMississippi. Over 1.5 inches of rain is possible near the Alabama\nstate line east of Tupelo, but the overall focus for the heaviest\nrainfall has shifted quite a bit farther south over the last\n24 hours. Areas along the central Gulf Coast could see over\n5 inches of rain. Thunderstorms are expected, but the threat of\nany severe storms looks minimal. Both dynamic and thermodynamic\nparameters are marginal at best, shear profiles are weak\n(<25 kts) and unidirectional, and extensive cloud cover will\nlimit any surface-based instability. CAPE values are maximized\nover west Tennessee and north Mississippi tomorrow afternoon, but\nare below 350 J/kg. Precipitable water values will climb to\nbetween 1.8 and 2.2 inches across the Mid-South tomorrow. As a\nresult, locally heavy rain is possible and could result in\nlocalized flooding, but antecedent conditions will help limit the\nflooding threat. Rainfall is not expected to alleviate the\nsubstantial rainfall deficits that are already occurring across\nthe area.\n\nWeak northwest flow will remain in place through the weekend and\nwell into the upcoming work week. A 1026 mb surface high will\nbuild over the Northern Plains and shift over the Great Lakes\nMonday and over New Jersey by midweek. The Mid-South will remain\non the southern periphery of the surface high, keeping us dry.\nThe coolest temperatures since around the middle of May will\narrive in the Mid-South by Tuesday. Low temperatures will be in\nthe upper 40s to middle 50s. High temperatures early next week\nwill be in the middle 70s to low 80s.",
